/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A pure substance is matter whose chemical composition is the same everywhere in the system.
   - A phase is a quantity of matter uniform in chemical composition and physical structure.
   - Saturation is the state at which a pure substance can change phase with no change in temperature or pressure.
   - Five names cover the behaviour.
   - Take 2 kg of water in a piston–cylinder at 101.325 kPa and 20 °C, and add heat slowly while the piston holds the pressure constant.
   - v = vf + x (vg − vf)
   - v is the specific volume of the wet mixture in m³/kg; vf and vg are the saturated liquid and saturated vapour specific volumes at the same pressure, in m³/kg; x is the dryness fraction, x = mg / m, the vapour mass divided by the total mass,
   - Figure 5.1.1 draws this path on the T-v plane, with v on a log scale so that 0.001 and 1.7 m³/kg fit on one page.
   - The state postulate says that for a simple compressible substance two independent intensive properties fix the state.
   - Flatten the surface onto the p-v plane and the wet region becomes the saturation dome, crossed by a horizontal line for each constant-pressure heating.
*/
