/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - On a pressure-temperature (p-T) diagram, a pure substance shows three phase boundaries.
   - At the triple point, solid, liquid and vapour coexist in equilibrium.
   - Below the triple-point pressure, liquid water cannot exist.
   - Figure 5.2.1 draws the three curves on a p-T diagram (not to scale) with the two heating paths of the worked example: one at 0.4 kPa, below the triple point, and one at atmospheric pressure.
   - The vaporisation curve ends at the critical point.
   - Above the critical temperature, no amount of pressure can liquefy the substance.
   - On a p-v diagram, the critical point is where the saturated liquid line and saturated vapour line meet.
   - The triple and critical points of a few common fluids are worth knowing, because the compressibility chart uses the critical values.
   - Engineers read the p-T diagram to choose the right phase for a process.
   - The triple point warns you about low-pressure processes.
*/
