/* book exam traps: Reading 0.6117 kPa as 0.6117 MPa. The triple-point pressure of water is about 0.006 atm, not 6 atm. A factor of 1000 puts you above atmospheric pressure and gives a completely different phase. | Using gauge pressure instead of absolute pressure. Gauges read zero at atmospheric pressure (101.325 kPa). The triple point and critical point are absolute pressures. | Thinking any gas can be liquefied by squeezing it. Above the critical temperature no pressure produces a separate liquid; carbon dioxide above 304.1 K stays one fluid however hard it is compressed. | Confusing the critical point with the triple point. The triple point has three phases; the critical point has one phase where liquid and vapour are indistinguishable. | Drawing water’s melting curve leaning to the right. Ice is less dense than liquid water, so water’s melting curve leans to the left: more pressure lowers the melting point slightly. Most other substances lean to the right. */
